  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front will bring widespread precipitation to the area \nWednesday night into Thursday. Models have backed off on the \nprecipitation amounts, especially over southeast Ohio and\nnorthwestern West Virginia where amounts now appear to be in the\ntwo tenths to half inch range. Other ares look to receive a\nthird to two thirds of an inch.\n\nThe coldest air of the season will move in behind the front for\nFriday into the weekend. Higher elevations of the mountains \nshould begin to mix with and change over to snow Thursday \nnight. Models previously had a closed low moving over the area\non Friday, but have now trended toward a short wave. This short\nwave will enhance precipitation on Friday. Friday night into \nSaturday, gusty winds and cold air advection will create upslope\nsnow conditions, even though 850 mb temperatures are not quite\nin the ideal range. The combination of these events could \nproduce several inches of accumulating snow over the higher \nelevations of the mountains. Boundary layer temperatures will \nremain marginal for snow in the lower elevation and the \nlowlands. If snow does occur in those locations, ground \ntemperatures should be too warm for any accumulations.",
